PRIVATE REFLECTION: The board is the 20×20 A–T × 1–20 Reversi board rendered with LaTeX grid lines. The only material is the SE staircase. Up to the unavoidable one-square LaTeX offset the occupied part is the familiar B/W staircase:

* F,G,H black on the top of the block; I15/I19 black with I16-I18 white, I20 black;
* J13,J14,J20 black with J15-J19 white;
* K15-K19, L15-L19 white;
* M16,N16 black with M17-M19, N17-N19 white;
* O17/O18/O19 black with O20 white, etc.

The longest elementary B W* B string that can be closed is the vertical string in files 17/18/19. In ordinary coordinates e.g. file 18:

H18 . – I18 W – J18 W – K18 W – L18 W – M18 W – N18 W – O18 B

Putting black on H18 closes I18…N18, six consecutive white men between the new black disc and O18. The symmetric strings I17…N17 and I19…N19 are identical. This is the textbook six-disc vertical flip and is the maximal one-direction capture.

The same square also closes a short diagonal. In matrix notation H18 – I19 W – J20 B, i.e. one extra white man on the SE diagonal. In the usual R C notation the move is N20:

* Horizontally N20 – N19 W – N18 W – N17 W – N16 B : 3 white men;
* NW-SE diagonal N20 – M19 W – L18 W – K17 W – J16 W – I15 B : 4 white men.

The two directions are disjoint, so the move turns over 3+4 = 7 distinct white discs, six of them on the long vertical line and one on the diagonal. Counting the newly played black stone the play is an 8-stone move.

All colleagues in round 1 now agree on this description up to coordinate naming. The earlier round disagreement about 9/12/13 discs is resolved as a grid mis-alignment; the robust figures are 6 white discs in the longest single line and 7 white discs total per legal black move, 8 stones including the move itself. No other empty square meets a black disc through a white interval in more than these two directions, so this is the maximal total flip.

